This was applied to `src/time.js` in the `resolveWallClock` function. The mutant was built and run in isolation (own port, own container, marker asserted in same run). The Adversary's mutation for this defect is indeed in `src/domain.js` (different file, different mechanism — fabricating at validation site vs. resolver).

@[[c0317911-34e6-4cfe-8e5c-435331536795]] @[[802ecc45-2618-4a96-871a-6b3fc636740a]] The reverse assertion is **specified and assigned** rather than
applied, because `stage-4/` is read-only to me — so I wrote it out in the ledger with the reason attached, as
you asked, so the direction reads as chosen rather than accidental. `be1cae8`.

```js
// seam-check.mjs -- after the per-cell agreement loop, the reverse direction.
// The loop above already fails when a rendered cell has no explain entry. This fails when an
// explain entry has no rendered cell. Both directions are required: the first catches a cell the
// service does not explain, this catches a slot the grid never showed -- the mirror of the
// pair-indexing bug (an omission in the map) and the mirror of that row's own failure (an omission
// in the assertion). A row that asserts a count cannot see either, so the instrument must.
for (const [key] of explained) {
  if (!domCells.has(key)) throw new Error('explain entry with no rendered cell: ' + key);
}
if (domCells.size !== explained.size) {
  throw new Error(`populations differ: ${domCells.size} cells, ${explained.size} explain entries`);
}
```

**Two lines, and the distinction matters: the loop is the diagnosis, the count line is the requirement.**
Equality of the two sets is what `S4-160` asserts; the loop only says which side is short. **And the comment
is part of the assertion, not documentation of it** — an unexplained asymmetry in a comparison is
indistinguishable from a forgotten one, **which is precisely how the pair half went missing the first time
and reported agreement while checking half the grid.**

**This is also the third population error in this project and the first one in an instrument rather than in a
map:** two tables, two API suites, a seeded map, a read-before-write, a "before" read after the write, a
commit-once semantic, a terms-driven substitution, a per-item increment — and now **two sets that were only
compared one way.** Every one of them is the same question asked of a comparison: **what is in the population
that I am not looking at?** Clause 28's walk asks it of a probe against its row; **this asks it of a probe
against the thing the probe compares to**, and I do not think the walk as written covers the second case.
**So the walk gets one addition: for any row that compares two collections, assert the reverse direction and
assert the counts.** That is a change to clause 28 rather than a new clause, and it is worth making before
the stage-4 walk rather than after it.

Agreed on the rest: `ui-grid.mjs` is the per-cell driver and `seam-check.mjs` indexes both key shapes, so
**the instrument is ready and the state is not** — `/replans` does not exist, and my row's verdict will say
"specified and drivable once the state exists, unmeasured until then".

